Deer play vital roles in the natural and cultural environment of New York and are highly valued for their beauty and grace as well as the utilitarian benefits they provide. However, the abundance of deer in many parts of the state is causing increasing problems, particularly in suburban and urban areas. This page and its sub-pages provide information on the origins and implications of this situation and various avenues for addressing these problems.

In response to this issue, in 2017 the New York State Legislature passed legislation requiring DEC to produce a [report on deer management techniques in urban and suburban areas](https://dec.ny.gov/docs/wildlife_pdf/decdeerreport18.pdf) (PDF). The law specified eight topics to be covered in the report:

1.   an evaluation of methods other than hunting that may be employed to reduce deer populations;
2.   methods to mitigate deer damage, including vehicle collisions;
3.   measures employed by DEC to minimize human-deer conflict;
4.   methods to enhance public engagement in and awareness of deer management issues;
5.   methods to reduce the spread of illnesses such as Lyme disease;
6.   identification of urban and suburban areas of the state with high deer densities;
7.   the basis for DEC's deer population target levels; and
8.   effective management measures employed in other states.

Because urban/suburban deer management is complex and influenced by a variety of socio-political and biological factors, DEC organized this report as a comprehensive review of the issue, providing additional information beyond that which was requested by the 2017 legislation.

